WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.784
A prescription was not finished when its ingredients were combined.

00:00:04.784 --> 00:00:09.018
In medieval Cairo, a medicine still had to be made reliable.

00:00:12.508 --> 00:00:17.751
The Minhaj al-dukkan associated with al-Kuhin al-Attar documents practical pharmaceutical

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:22.993
choices. It is an historical text, not a modern clinical trial.

00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:32.002
Concentration, ingredients and preservation are questions of practice. A formula

00:00:32.002 --> 00:00:36.996
means little if the preparation changes before it reaches its user.

00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:46.094
A pharmacist with an unavailable ingredient had to consider replacement.

00:00:46.094 --> 00:00:51.305
Similar names or therapeutic intentions did not guarantee material equivalence.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.828
Formulary instructions and documentary traces allow an investigation of pharmaceutical reliability,

00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:06.355
but they do not establish the clinical success of each recipe.

00:01:10.508 --> 00:01:15.581
This forthcoming historical article asks how pharmaceutical dependability was

00:01:15.581 --> 00:01:20.654
achieved and described under the conditions of Ayyubid-Mamluk Cairo.
